      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "A new 22-lead electrocardiographic test has been advocated as a screening tool for coronary artery disease and has been shown to have accuracy similar to stress electrocardiography in specific patient populations."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine the accuracy of this test for detecting coronary artery disease in patients undergoing coronary angiography for a variety of cardiac conditions."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We prospectively determined the temporal electrical heterogeneity (TEH) index at rest in 70 patients who had no angina or Q waves on the resting 12-lead electrocardiogram before they underwent coronary angiography."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Twenty-six of the 70 patients had significant coronary artery disease, defined as 70% stenosis or greater in at least one major epicardial coronary artery. A TEH index of 80 or more had a sensitivity of 58%, a specificity of 75%, and a positive predictive value of 58%. The group with significant coronary disease had a mean TEH index of 77.2, and the group without coronary disease had a mean index of 65.5 (P = .02), despite similar clinical characteristics and indications for angiography."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The TEH index shows promise as a screening tool for coronary artery disease in a heterogeneous cardiac population. However, larger studies are needed before it can be endorsed for widespread clinical use."
        }
      ]},
